Be sure to read the <a href="#Rules">Rules</a> if you have not already.  Also in addition to this page, be sure to check out the user-run, <? echo $html->link('Minethings wiki', 'http://wiki.minethings.com'); ?>.  If you'd like an account there, contact support@minethings.com.

<h3 id="Mining_Things">Mining Things</h3>

<p>You can't help but mine things.  You are always mining, day and night whether logged into minethings.com or not.  You never know when you will discover something.  </p>

<p>To see what you've discovered, just click "Mines."  If you have found anything new, it will be listed here immediately.  Note that you must click "Mines" in order to see the new things you've found.  Clicking "Things" will only show you what you have since you last checked your mines.</p>

<h3 id="Mining_Gold">Mining Gold</h3>

<p>You can mine gold if you'd prefer gold instead of things.  Just go to your "Mines" page and click the button that says "mine gold."  Gold findings vary in size; usually, you will get about 3g at a time but sometimes you find much more.  All mines generate gold at the same average rate.</p>

<h3 id="Batteries">Batteries</h3>

<p>Your miner bots run on batteries and when the batteries run out, they will stop mining.  The battery time remaining is shown at the top of each page.</p>

<p>To recharge the batteries, click "Mines."  They will be instantly recharged to last 20 hours plus one hour for each meld that you have.  </p>

<h3 id="Mine_Faster">How to Mine Faster</h3>

<p>1. Obtain equipment.  These are things like the drills, pickaxes, lights and hardhats that boost your mining rate.  You can find these at an equipment mine if you own one, or you can buy them from other players.  A miner bot can use one of each type of equipment.  You can browse equipment at the <? echo $html->link('Equipment Mine', '/mine_types/browse/4'); ?></p>

<p>2. Clear stones.  Each stone you see on the Mines page is slowing you down.  Hover your mouse over a stone to see what you need to do to clear it.  For each one that you clear out, you'll mine half a bucker per hour faster at the top mine of your home city.  

<p>3. Purchase or rent additional mines.  You will mine from these new mines at the same base rate as your starting mine (25 buckets per hour).  You can mine from up to three different mines at once.  If you have two mines, you will mine twice as many things.  If you have three mines, you will mine three times as many things.  You can buy more than three mines but will only be able to mine from three at one time.  You can purchase mines for gold or credits at the <? echo $html->link('Shop', '/credits/shop'); ?>.</p>

<p>4. Purchase <? echo $html->link('Explosives', '/mine_types/browse/11'); ?> from other players.  These can be detonated at any mine (even rentals) and will perform hours worth of mining instantly.  M-80s typically find 0-1 things, Dynamite 1-3 things and TNT 5-9 things.  The rarer explosives deliver even more.  

<p>5. Purchase <? echo $html->link('Mining Robots', '/mine_types/browse/17'); ?> from other players.  These will find damaged things of rarity blue and higher in addition to your regular findings.  The higher the model number, the more damaged things your bot will find.</p>

<p>6. Purchase <? echo $html->link('Gadgets', '/mine_types/browse/10'); ?> such as the Hammer and the Remote Control.  The hammer boosts all equipment you own and the remote control allows you to use a fourth mine.</p>

<h3 id="Melds">Melds</h3>

<p>A meld is a collection of things.  Each type of meld requires its own specific set of things and a specific quantity of each.  If you have the required things in your home city, you can make the meld.  Each meld can only be created once per miner.  Your level (number next to your name) is determined by how many melds you have.  Some melds are harder to get than others.</p>

<p>Each meld adds one hour to your battery life.  Melds also provide increasing bonuses depending on what profession you choose.  Bum fights are resolved by meld count.  Workers gain productivity as they gain more melds.  Vehicles receive a bonus to their armor.  Ships gain additional hull.  Pilots can fly more aircraft and pipe oil faster.  Manufacturers produce better-quality robots.</p> 

<p>To create a meld, go to the <? echo $html->link('Market', '/mine_types/browse/1'); ?> page, click on one of the melds found under the Melds heading, and then click "Create Meld."  If you have the required things, you will see a message telling you that the meld has been created and your meld count will increase by one.</p>

<p>The Starter mine tends to have the easiest melds but other mines have melds as well.  Have a look at all the mines to see which melds will be the easiest for you to make next.</p>

<h3 id="Buying">Buying</h3>

<p>Gold can be used to buy things from other miners.  To buy something, the first thing to do is locate the page for that thing.  Click on the "Market" link and then click on the type of mine your desired thing is found in.  You will see a list of all known things from that mine.  Click on the thing you are interested in and you will be taken to the market page for that thing.</p>

<p>You may find that there are people who are willing to sell you this thing right away.  Their prices will be found under the "Listings" column.  If there are listings, you can click the "Buy" link and you will be able to purchase immediately.  </p>

<p>If there are no listings or if the listings are too high in price, you have the option to bid on a thing.  To do so, simply click "Buy," enter the amount of gold you'd like to spend on the next page, and click "Place Bid."  Your bid will be added to the "Bids" column and will stay there until either you cancel it or someone sells to you.  Keep in mind that there are other bidders also interested in the same thing and the higher your bid, the sooner your purchase will generally be made.  Once someone does meet your bid, you will receive a message to let you know. </p>

<h3 id="Selling">Selling</h3>

<p>Selling works similarily to buying.  To sell one of your things, first locate the market page.  You can do this from your "Things" page by finding the thing you wish to sell and clicking its name.  You will be taken to the market page for this thing.  </p>

<p>You might find that there are bidders already lined up waiting to purchase at your command.  These bids will be listed in the "Bids" column.  If the price is right, click the "Sell" and then "Sell for..." on the next page.  You will immediately sell to the highest bidder.</p>

<p>If there are no bids or if you don't like the bids that people have placed, you can place a listing instead.  Just click the "Sell" link, type in any price you'd like and click "Place Listing."  Your listing will be added to the "Listings" column and at some point somone else may come around and buy yours.  There may be others who are also trying to sell the same thing and you will sell yours sooner if your price is lower.  You will get a message once the sale has been made.</p>

<h3 id="Inventory_Limit">Inventory Limit</h3>

<p>You start out with an inventory limit of 50 things.  Your inventory is computed as the total count of all mined, bought, manufactured or fished things belonging to you.  Things en-route and in other cities count towards this inventory.  Mines, melds, attached cannons, installed mods/weapons, and factories do not.  </p>

<p>If you are over your inventory limit, you will not be able to bring new findings from your mines into your inventory.  You will continue mining at the same rate and accumulating new findings, but you cannot see what they are or use them until reducing your inventory.  If you are 100 things over your inventory limit, you are at risk of having your excess inventory stolen by Bums.  You also cannot use vehicles while being 100 over inventory.</p>

<p>To reduce your inventory, click 'Things,' click on one of your things and either sell or trash it.  </p>

<h3 id="Thing_Rarity">Thing Rarity</h3>

<p>The color of the thing represents its rarity.  Things in the "Market" tab are listed top-bottom from most to least rare (orange/purple/red/blue/green/yellow).  Each color is many times more common than the color above it.  Rarity alone does not determine the price of a thing but it plays a large part.  There are some things which do not conform to this rarity system; they are colored grey.</p>

<h3 id="Cities">Cities</h3>

<p>Your home city has a lot going on and you can progress in the game without ever venturing out, but it is only one corner of the world.  There are other cities with different mines you can access if you buy a vehicle or a ship.  A vehicle/ship also allows you to transport your things from one city to another.  You can buy vehicles/ships from other players just the same way you buy anything else.</p>

<p>When you first start your account, you can only see your home city.  To view another city, you must first send a vehicle/ship to that city.  Once you have visited the city once, it will appear in your "Cities" menu and you will be able to view that city whenever you want.</p>

<p>Each city has its own marketplace and you'll find that prices differ from city to city.  A thing's market page shows the bids and listings for the current city only.  If you want to see market data for another city, simply change the current city via the "Cities" menu found at the top of every page.  </p>

<p>Each city has its own set of mines.  This list can be found by clicking "Shop" and noting which mines are listed for sale.  To see another city's mines, click "Cities" and then the city you want.</p>

<p>Melds can only be created in your home city.  You can buy the things needed from any city you want, but in order to make a meld with them they must be in your home city.</p>

<h3 id="Moving">Moving</h3>

<p>Moving means changing your home city.  It doesn't change which mines you mine from, but the top mine in your home city receives a mining bonus based on how many stones you have cleared.  It also changes the city where you must bring things in order to transform them into melds.  </p>

<p>Some professions are affected by your choice of home city.  Bums only steal inventory in their home city.  Workers can only work in their home city.  Manufacturers can only own factories in their home city.</p>

<p>To move to another city, first you must change to that city by clicking the "Cities" dropdown menu and clicking on the city you want.  Once you are switched to the city you want to move to, click "Home" and you will see a link on that page that says "Move to [city name]."  </p>

<p>Moving does come with its costs.  Upon moving, <b>all your melds will become nullified.</b>  That means that your meld count will go back to zero.  These null melds are located in your former home city and you can dismantle each one of them back into the things that were required to make that meld.  You cannot move again if you have null melds.</p>

<p>Another cost is that you will no longer have the mining bonus in your former home city from clearing stones.  This "home city" bonus moves as you do and will apply only to one mine in your new home city.  So if you only have a starter mine and move away from the starting city, you will lose that bonus.  Generally, it's best to first have a mine in the city to which you are moving.</p>

<p>Since you will be below the minimum required melds for choosing most professions, your profession will revert back to Bum.  Vehicles/ships/aircraft en-route will retain their profession until arrival.  You may switch back to the same profession while they are en route if you acquire the number of melds required for that profession.</p>

<p>Once you move, it is required that you stay in your new home for at least one week. </p>

<h3 id="Gadgets">Gadgets</h3>

<p>Many different types of gadgets are available to give you various boosts.  You can buy them from other players at the <? echo $html->link('Gadget', '/mine_types/browse/10'); ?> mine or purchase a gadget mine and find them yourself.  Some gadgets give boosts to vehicles, ships and aircraft while others give you extra information such as sales history, market notices, and meld costs.  While gadgets are not essential to advance in minethings, they will give you a noticeable benefit. </p>

<p>To use a gadget, first click on the <? echo $html->link('Gadgets', '/gadgets'); ?> link on your left-hand menu.  There you'll see all gadgets you own or have activated.  Gadgets in your inventory will not benefit you until you activate them from this page.  To activate a gadget, click "Add N days."  If this button is greyed out, it means that the gadget is not in your home city.  It must be in your home city to activate.</p>

<p>Gadgets will remain functional until their expiration.  Each additional time you activate a gadget, more time will be added to the gadget's duration extending its use.  So while you cannot stack gadgets together to get a higher net boost, you can extend the duration of the boost.</p>

<h3 id="Vehicles">Vehicles</h3>

<p>Vehicles allow you to visit other cities, transport things, pillage other player's cargo and hunt for highwaymen.  You can buy a vehicle from another player if there are any listed in your city.  Go to the "Market" page and click Vehicles to find your first vehicle.  </p>

<p>Vehicles have four attributes: speed, capacity, attack and armor.  Speed determines how quickly you move along each route and also determines whether combat occurs in the event that you are chasing or being chased.  Capacity determines how many things you can carry along the route.  Attack wears down your opponent's armor and armor lets you sustain more of your opponent's attacks.  Some vehicles have very little attack and armor and rely on mods and weapons to win battles.</p>

<p>If you choose either the highwayman or guard profession, you can have your vehicles pillage or hunt selected rarities of vehicles.  Doing so puts you in an aggressive stance towards those vehicles.  Combat takes place when two vehicles meet and one aggressive vehicle catches the other.</p>

<h3 id="Vehicle_Combat">Vehicle Combat</h3>

<p>Highwaymen and Guards have the option to be aggressive towards other vehicles by clicking "hunt" or "pillage" upon send.  Highwaymen selecting this option are aggressive towards Traders of the selected colors.  Guards selecting this option are aggressive towards all Highwaymen of the selected colors.  Either profession may choose to also be aggressive towards Guards of the selected colors.</p>

<p>When an aggressive vehicle encounters a non-aggressive vehicle, the first thing to happen is that the aggressive vehicle chases the other.  If the non-aggressive vehicle is faster, it escapes.  Otherwise, a battle ensues lasting approximately 30 minutes.  The winner is determined by trading blows round by round until one vehicle's armor is reduced to zero.</p>

<p>Generally, armor is reduced each round by the opponent's attack.  The aggressor's undodged offense is also subtracted from the defender's armor each round while the defender's undodged defense reduces the aggressor's attack each round (to a minimum of 0.5).  Here, "undodged" means whatever offense or defense is left after subtracting dodge (minimum 0).  As an example:</p>

<p>Highwayman: (Attack:3.5, Armor:5, Offense:4, Defense:2, Dodge:5) vs </p>
<p>Trader: (Attack:1.2, Armor:10, Offense:6, Defense:6.5, Dodge:3)</p>
<p>Highwayman's undodged offense = 1</p>
<p>Trader's undodged defense = 1.5</p>
<ul>
<li>Round 1: Trader hurts Highwayman attack with an undodged defense of 1.5.  Highwayman has attack of 2 and undodged offense of 1 and hits Trader for 3 damage (7 armor).  Trader hits Highwayman for 1.2 damage (3.8 armor).</li>
<li>Round 2: Trader hurts Highwayman attack with an undodged defense of 1.5.  Highwayman has an attack of 0.5 and undodged offense of 1 and hits Trader for 1.5 damage (5.5 armor).  Trader hits Highwayman for 1.2 damage (2.6 armor).</li>
<li>Round 3: Trader can not hurt Highwayman attack any further.  Highwayman has an attack of 0.5 and undodged offense of 1 and hits Trader for 1.5 damage (4 armor).  Trader hits Highwayman for 1.2 damage (1.4 armor).</li>
<li>Round 4: Highwayman has an attack of 0.5 and undodged offense of 1 and hits Trader for 1.5 damage (2.5 armor).  Trader hits Highwayman for 1.2 damage (0.2 armor).</li>
<li>Round 5: Highwayman has an attack of 0.5 and undodged offense of 1 and hits Trader for 1.5 damage (1 armor).  Trader hits Highwayman for the final blow of 1.2 damage (0 armor).</li>
</ul>
<p>Trader wins.  As you can see, Trader was not able to make use of their offense and Highwayman was unable to use their defense.  Each could have been better off had they brought different weapons.  </p>

<p>If an aggressive Guard meets an aggressive Highwayman, there is no chase and they take turns being aggressor/defender.  The first aggressor is chosen randomly.</p>

<p>Note that battle information is not so detailed in-game.  Some opponent stats will have to be estimated based on the limited information available on the battle screen.</p>

<p>Vehicle combat for non-yellow vehicles is also affected by a meld-based armor boost.  The more melds the owner has, the more armor their vehicle will have.</p>

<h3>After Combat</h3>

<p>The losing vehicle of any battle can be pillaged if the other vehicle is aggressive.  Pillaging means that the victor takes ownership of whatever is in the victim's vehicle and places those things in their own vehicle.  If the aggressor doesn't have room for the victim's things, the aggressor will only take enough things to fill their cargo. 

<p>Anything a vehicle is carrying can be pillaged, including loaded oil.  If the victim has non-weapon cargo and the victor has space, random non-weapon cargo will be moved to the victor's vehicle until it's full.  If instead there's no non-weapon cargo or no space for cargo and the victim has oil loaded, the victor will take up to 5 trips of oil.  If neither of the above happens and the victor has space, one random weapon or mod will be pillaged.  At most one weapon/mod can be lost per vehicle per trip. </p>

<p>If a Trader or defensive vehicle wins the battle, no pillaging is done. </p>

<H3 id="Ships">Ships</H3>

<p>Ships are available to miners who have earned 10 or more melds.  They allow travel to offshore cities and can reach locations that vehicles alone cannot.  Ships can carry anything that vehicles can carry and behave very much like vehicles when traveling.  The differences are to be found in the ship combat.  Ships fight using a combination of cannons, crew, and weapons while vehicles use weapons and mods.  Vehicles are never lost altogether in combat but ships can be sunk.</p>

<p>Five attributes govern how a ship behaves: speed, capacity, cannon portals, crew and hull.  Speed for ships is just like speed for vehicles.  A ship's capacity determines how many things it can carry.  Cannon portals determines the maximum number of cannons that can be outfitted onto the ship.  Hull determines how many cannonballs a ship can absorb before it is sunk.  The hull of non-yellow ships is further boosted according to the meld count of its owner.</p>  

<p>To use a ship, you must first switch professions to either Merchant, Pirate, Bounty Hunter or Fisherman.  The nature of these professions is described <a href="#Professions">here</a>.  Once you have switched to one of these professions, click on the Ships link on the left-hand menu and you will see your ships listed ready to send.  Sending ships works just like vehicles with the exception of cannons and cannonballs.</p>

<p>Cannons are found in the Cannon mine and can be attached to a ship when the ship is sent.  Once attached, cannons can only be removed via pillaging or with a Block and Tackle.  A cannoned ship cannot be sold or trashed.  Each attached cannon will subtract 1 from the ship's capacity.  Alternatively, cannons can be brought onto the ship as cargo and will not be attached or used in battle, but can be stolen as cargo.  </p>

<p>Cannon ammo is created by Manufacturers at factories and there are three different types.  Cannonballs damage the enemy ship's hull in hopes of sinking the ship.  Each point of damage subtracts 1 from the enemy ship's hull.  Grape shot is aimed at the enemy's crew to cause casualties and turn the crew battle in your favor.  Each point of damage will cause 1 crew member to fall.  Chain-shot tears the enemy's sails, slowing the ship and allowing yours to escape.  Each point of damage reduces the enemy ship's speed by 1 km/hr.  Cannon ammo can be brought onboard the ship in crates, each crate occupying one cargo space.  Crates that are partially used will remain on-board until empty and take no cargo space.</p>

<p>Ships can be sunk if their hull reaches zero.  A sunken ship is lost forever but its cargo remains intact at the bottom of the sea, ready for Fishermen to find.</p>

<h3 id="Ship_Combat">Ship Combat</h3>

<p>Like vehicles, combat occurs when one two ships pass one another and one of them is instructed to hunt or pillage the class of the other.  Pirates are aggressive towards Merchants and Fishermen and are optionally aggressive towards Bounty Hunters (just like vehicles).  If the defensive ship is faster, it escapes before combat occurs.</p>

<p>Combat between ships occurs in two phases.  In phase one, the cannons of both ships are fired and damage is taken on both sides.  In phase two, the crew of each ship fights one another.  </p>

<p>The cannon phase is fought over three rounds.  Only cannons that have been permanently attached are used in this phase.  Cannons brought on as cargo are ignored.  Some cannons fire in all three rounds while other cannons will only fire in one or two rounds.  The rounds a cannon will fire in is determined by the cannon's rate of fire. </p>

<p>Each round begins with both ships firing their cannons in portal 1 if they can.  The cannon ammo is chosen randomly from those aboard the ship.  Damage is tallied up on each side and two important calculations are made: if the defender becomes significantly faster due to damaging the other's sails, the ship will escape immediately.  Also if a ship's hull reaches zero, the ship is sunk and all cargo is lost.  Then both sides fire from portal 2 if they can, then both from 3, 4, 5, etc until they have both fired all their cannons.  At this point, the next round begins.</p> 

<p>The crew battle phase follows the cannon phase.  Some crew may have been lost during the cannon phase but the remaining crew will still fight valiantly.  Each crew member will grab a weapon if there are enough available on the ship.  The weapons will add to the crew strength.  If there are more weapons than crew, the best weapons will be chosen and the rest left aside.  Crew strength is calculated as the crew count plus the total offense+defense of the weapons they are carrying.  If a crew member falls, their weapon stats are also removed from the crew's total strength.  </p>

<p>The fight will commence by randomly choosing one crew member from each ship and comparing their weapons.  The crew member with the more common weapon falls, subtracting himself and the weapon from the crew's total strength.  If both crew members have the same weapon power, a winner is chosen randomly.  This repeats until the strength of one crew is significantly greater than the strength of the other, at which point the losing side capitulates.<p>

<p>A ship that loses hull, crew or speed during battle can regain the lost stats.  Immediately after battle, most of the hull and sail damage is repaired and most of the crew casualties are revived.  Upon arriving at a city, sails are repaired fully and the crew is replenished with fresh recruits.  The remaining hull damage requires some time to repair and this will happen automatically as long as the ship is in port.</p>

<p>Anything a ship is carrying can be pillaged except loaded ammo.  If the victim has non-weapon cargo and the victor has space, random non-weapon cargo will be moved to the victor's ship until it's full.  If that doesn't happen and the victim has oil loaded, the victor will take up to 5 trips of oil.  If neither of the above happens and the victor has space, one random weapon or cannon will be pillaged.  At most one weapon/cannon can be lost per ship per trip (unless the ship is sunk). </p>

<h3 id="Classes">Vehicle and Ship Classes</h3>

<p>Vehicles and ships are grouped by class according to their rarity.  Yellow vehicles and ships are in the lowest class.  Greens and blues are in the second class.  Reds, Purples and Oranges are in the top class.  A vehicle/ship can only fight vehicles/ships in their own class.  So this way, a player with an orange cannot beat up a new player who just has a yellow.  Oranges can only fight reds, purples and oranges.  A green can only fight greens and blues.  A yellow can only fight other yellows.  </p>

<p>While all vehicles have a rating, only vehicles/ships of the top class affect a player's rank (the rank is the colored badge you see next to a player's name). </p>

<p>Classes also affect what a vehicle/ship can carry as cargo.  Yellows can only carry yellows.  Greens/blues can only carry greens/blues.  Reds/purples/oranges can only carry reds/purples/oranges/greys.  <b>Exceptions</b> green/blue can carry Oil and any fisherman ship can carry Lures and yellow fish.</p>

<p>The rule for carrying arms (weapons, cannons, ammo, and mods) is different.  Yellows can carry grey/yellow arms.  Greens/blues can carry grey/yellow/green/blue arms.  Reds/purples/oranges can carry any arms they want. </p>

<h3 id="Aircraft">Aircraft</h3>

<p>Aircraft are used by those of the Pilot profession to find, destroy, and pillage the ore thieves' base.  Aircraft can fly to any city but ore thief missions are flown only from the city where ore is mined.  They are sent just like vehicles and ships and behave similarly.  </p>

<p>There are three types of aircraft:</p>

<p><b>Search Planes</b> are sent out to find the ore thieves' base.  Upon finding the base, they will radio its position and then try not to get shot down.  If your Search Plane is the first to find the base, its location will be your knowledge alone.  However, others' search planes may also find the base later on and compete to recover the ore.</p>

<p><b>Bombers</b> are used to destroy the ore theives' base and can be sent once its location is known to the pilot.  Bombers can be sent empty but in order to do any damage, they must carry bombs.  All bombs brought will be dropped on the thieves' base and if it is destroyed, helicopters are then free to land and recover the ore.  However, if any others also know of its location, they will be allowed to pillage the base after its destruction as well.  Bombers can be shot down by the ore thieves.  </p>

<p><b>Helicopters</b> are used to recover ore from the thieves once their base has been destroyed.  They cannot land if the base is still intact upon their arrival and will attempt to return home immediately instead.  If the base is destroyed when they arrive, helicopters will take as many ore crates as their capacity allows and attempt to return home.  In either case, they may be shot down by vengeful thieves hiding in the woods.  Helicopters also help Pilots deploy <a href="#Machines">machines</a>.</p>

<p>Demolishing the ore thieves' base is an expensive undertaking but can bring great rewards in return.  The ore thieves do not learn from their mistakes and will continue stealing ore and building new bases, providing pilots with an everlasting incentive to search and destroy.  </p>

<h3 id="Weapons">Weapons</h3>

<p>Weapons help your vehicles attack and defend while they are traveling and they help your ship's crew in battle.</p>

<p><b>Vehicles:</b> Each weapon has an offense and defense rating which are used along with the vehicle stats to determine the outcome of an attack.  The offensive rating is used when your vehicle is attacking another vehicle.  The defensive rating is used for when your vehicle is being attacked by another. </p>

<p><b>Ships:</b> Your crew can arm themselves with weapons during the crew vs crew phase of battle.  Each weapon that is carried by a crew member will add both its offense and defense stats to your crew numbers.  If there aren't enough crew to use the weapons on your ship, the best weapons will be chosen.  If a crew member is killed/wounded in battle, their weapon is dropped and no longer counts towards the crew strength.</p>

<p>To use a weapon, simply bring it along when you are selecting which things you want your vehicle/ship to carry.  Weapons occupy one cargo slot each.  Their stats are automatically added to a vehicle's stats and automatically used by a ship's crew.  If you'd like to attach a weapon to a vehicle so that it takes no inventory space, you can do so by clicking [weapons] from the vehicle's status screen.  The weapon will still occupy one cargo space on the vehicle but won't count against your global inventory limit.</p>

<p>You can find weapons at the <? echo $html->link('weapons mine', '/mine_types/browse/7'); ?> or purchase them in any city.</p>


<h3 id="Banking">Banking</h3>

<p>The banking tab on the left provides a way in which players can lend money to each other.  "Banker" is the profession that facilitates these loans.  To make any loans or deposits through this system, you must negotiate with a banker.</p>

<p><b>Loans</b> are initiated when a customer applies for one.  This is done by clicking Banking, then "Apply for a loan."  You decide how much gold you want (principle), how long you want it for (duration) and what monthly interest you are willing to pay (compounded daily).  As you fill out this form, you will notice three values changing on their own.  These are the number of weekly payments you will make, the amount you will need to pay each week, and the total cost of the loan. </p>

<p>When you've finished deciding the terms, choose which banks you'd like to send the application to.  You can send it to as many banks as you would like.  If one bank accepts an application, the other applications are automatically deleted.</p>

<p>Once you've sent the application, the bankers will be notified and respond to the application either by approving it, modifying the terms (duration/interest) or rejecting it completely.  If they modify the terms, you will be notified and you'll get a chance to either approve/modify/reject the application yourself.  Loan application modifications can go back and forth as many times as necessary for both parties to be satisfied with the terms.</p>

<p><b>Deposits</b> are very similar to loans.  A deposit is made when a customer wants to save his/her money at a bank.  This is essentially a loan going the other direction.  The main difference is that instead of being repaid on a weekly schedule, a deposit is paid back at the end of its duration.  </p>

<p>Customers should choose carefully who they deposit their money with, as a banker could decide to not repay the deposit at all.  Customers should research their bank carefully by clicking on the Banks sub-tab within the Banking page.	 Bankers need to exercise the same sort of caution when approving loans.</p>

<p>To help indicate a player's credit-worthiness, late payments are kept track of and are available to any bank at which a customer applies.  "Late days" are accumulated each day that a loan or deposit is overdue.  If the count reaches 90 days, then the loan is considered to have been defaulted and will stop accumulating late days.</p>

<p>Also available is a banker's join date, gold balance, and total banking assets/liabilities.  "Assets" means "what's owed to the banker" and is calculated as the total balance on all loans at their bank plus all personal deposits at other banks.  "Liabilities" means "what the banker owes" and is calculated as the total balance on all deposits at their bank plus all personal loans from other banks.  </p>


<H3 id="Professions">Professions</h3>

<p>There are twelve professions you can choose from.  Your profession determines whether you can use vehicles, ships and factories and how your vehicles and ships behave while moving from one city to another.  Everyone starts out as the Bum profession and cannot use vehicles/ships/factories.  There are three vehicle professions: (Trader, Highwayman, Guard), four ship professions: (Merchant, Pirate, Bounty Hunter, Fisherman), two factory professions: (Worker and Manufacturer) and Banker.  All professions mine at the same rate.</p>  

<p><B>Bums</B> are the only profession that can mine gold at a continuous rate.  They also keep hoarders in check by randomly stealing hoarders' loots at various times throughout the day (this happens on its own without any input from players).  A hoarder is someone who is more than 100 things over their inventory limit.  Bums always pick on the hoarder with the most things and start by stealing all the hoarder's yellows in the bum's home city.  If they have room and the hoarder has no more yellows in any city, the bums move onto the greens, then blues, etc.  If two bums happen to be picking through the same hoarder's things, they will fight.  The bum with the most melds takes home the loot.</p>

<p><B>Workers</B> are hired by other players on a weekly basis to work in factories in their home city.  They can help with building a factory or with any task that the factory is working on.  Their productivity is determined by how many melds they have and is measured in components per hour.  Every 10 melds gives them another +1 component per hour with no upper limit.  Workers cannot change professions if they are working for someone else.</p>

<p><B>Traders</B> can bring things on board their vehicles as cargo.  The only things they cannot bring are vehicles, ships and things outside of their vehicle's class (see <a href="#Classes">Classes</a> for more info).  Traders can earn gold by buying something in one city and selling it in another.  This earns them a profit based on the price difference between the two cities.  Traders do have to consider that there are Highwaymen who can engage them in battle and steal their cargo, weapons and mods. </p>

<p><B>Highwaymen</B> can only bring weapons and installed mods on vehicles and these must be part of their vehicle's <a href="#Classes">arms class</a>.  They chase Traders (and optionally, Guards) of the selected color and upon catching one will attempt to defeat it.  If the Highwayman's vehicle is victorious, it will steal all of the oil and all of the cargo it can fit.  Highwaymen choose upon sending to flee any Guards they encounter.  They might choose to do so because a Guard who defeats them will pillage their vehicle.</p>

<p><B>Guard</B> vehicles chase Highwaymen (and optionally Guards), fight the ones they can catch and pillage upon victory.  Guards are restricted to bringing only weapons and installed mods in their vehicles.</p>

<p><B>Fishermen</B> use bait and ships to catch fish.  Pieces of bait on a fisherman's ship are replaced with fish of equal rarity as the ship moves toward its destination.  Their bait will occasionally find sunken treasure instead.  Their ships are limited to carrying bait, fish, weapons and cannonballs, respecting <a href="#Classes">class</a>.</p>

<p><B>Merchants</B> are the sea's equivalent of Traders and can bring things on board their ships as cargo.  The only things they cannot bring are vehicles, ships, and things outside their <a href="#Classes">class</a>.  Merchants can take advantage of this unique ability by buying something in one city and selling it in another.  This earns them a profit based on the price difference between the two cities.  Merchants do have to consider that there are Pirates who can engage them in battle and steal their things. </p>

<p><B>Pirates</B> behave similarly to highwaymen except they use ships instead of vehicles.  Their ships are restricted to bringing only weapons and cannon ammo.  Pirates attack and pillage defensive ships while optionally attacking or running from aggressive Bounty Hunters.  A pirate who sinks an enemy's ship will get nothing.  Pirates must watch out for Bounty Hunters who can pillage their cargo/cannons/weapons.  </p>

<p><B>Bounty Hunters</B> behave similarly to Guards except they use ships instead of vehicles.  If a BH ship is aggressive and wins, it pillages the opponent's.  Bounty Hunter ships are restricted to bringing only weapons and cannon ammo, respecting <a href="#Classes">class</a>.</p>

<p><b>Pilots</b> Spend most of their days in their helicopters deploying <a href="#Machines">machines</a> and retrieving the extracted oil.  They can also fly aircraft to find, destroy, and recover ore from the ore thieves' base.  They have three types of aircraft at their disposal as well as a selection of bombs.  The number of aircraft that they can fly at once is limited by their meld count.  Every 10 melds allows them one more aircraft in the air.</p>

<p><B>Manufacturers</B> build, operate and rent out factories in their home city.  Their factories can be used to make mining robots, ore melds, aircraft, bombs, ammo, or repair damaged things.  The robots they build are equipped with sensors to detect and extract damaged things in addition to the normal findings.  The mining robot model that they build is determined by their meld count; the more melds they have, the more advanced the bot.  Manufacturers can also rent out their factories to others for the purpose of repairing damaged things.  Their factories can only be built in their home city and they can build at most five.  They need workers in order to accomplish anything and they cannot work while they are manufacturers.  Manufacturers cannot change professions or move if they have a factory that is either being built, working on a task, or rented out.</p>

<p><B>Bankers</B> lend gold to and accept gold deposits from other players.  They charge interest on loans and pay interest on deposits, earning money on the difference between.  Loans are risky to make because there is nothing forcing a player to repay them (the same is true of deposits).  Reputation is important and a banker must be careful who he trusts.  Melds help bankers by putting their bank's name ahead of anyone with fewer melds.</p>

<h3 id="Factories">Factories</h3>

<p>Factories are used to build and repair things.  They can make mining robots, cannon ammo, block and tackles, ore melds, and they can repair damaged things.  Factories are built only by Manufacturers but can be rented and operated by any profession to repair things.  Ore is required to build and operate factories.  Factory owners cannot move unless their factories are idle and not rented out.</p>

<p><B>Ore:</B>
In some mines, the option to mine gold is replaced by an option to mine ore.  Ore is required to build factories and to operate them.  Ore thieves live near the ore mines and will steal most ore as it is found.  Some of this ore can be recovered by pilots if their base is found and destroyed.  Ore can also be purchased from other players in the marketplace.</p>

<p><B>Components:</B>
Components are units of labor provided by workers and are required to build and operate factories.  Each task available on the factory page has a cost in components and once the task is started, up to seven workers can be assigned to it.  They will work at this task until either the operator makes them idle again, the task is canceled or until their contract expires.  Workers not working on a task do not produce components at all.  If a worker is taken off a task, the components that this worker has achieved are not lost and another worker can take their place and continue the work.</p>

<p><B>Mining Robot:</B>
Miner Robots come in many different models, with higher numbers indicating better detection ability.  These Miner bots are upgraded versions of the free bots you have working at your mines.  Free bots only find whole, undamaged things and consider the rest dirt.  The new mining robots also recognize things that were damaged in the eruption and will deliver these to you in addition to your normal findings, but only if the mine is set to mine things.  These damaged things must be repaired at a factory in order to use them.  The model number of the bot indicates its damaged finding rate and manufacturers with 50-59 melds will build MR5s.  Those with 60-69 melds build MR6s, etc.  There is no upper limit to the bot number other than the number of melds available to earn.  Mining robots can be sold or transported just like equipment and will automatically work at the top-priority mine in the city where they are placed.</p>

<p><B>Ore Melds:</B>
Factories can be used to create ore melds.  These melds behave very similarly to regular thing-based melds except that they can only be built at a factory and by the manufacturer and owner of the factory.  If the manufacturer moves after obtaining an ore meld, the meld is replaced with the ore that was required to build it.  The components are lost.  </p>

<p><b>Aircraft & Bombs:</b> Factories can make three types of aircraft (Search Plane, Bomber, Helicopter) and two types of bombs (Flower, Thumper).  Helicopters are used by pilots to deploy <a href="#Machines">machines</a> and retrieve oil.  Aircraft are also used by pilots to find, destroy, and pillage the ore theives' base.  Although these missions are  flown out of specific cities, aircraft and bombs can be made in any city and then flown to the required city by a pilot.  

<p><b>Cannon Ammo:</b> Cannon ammo is produced in factories in lots of 15 boxes.  Each box contains 8 crates with 12 shots per crate.</p>

<p><b>Block and Tackle:</b> Use this to strip a ship of all its cannons.  A cannons are a strain on the ropes so the Block and Tackle is destroyed upon use.</p>

<p><B>Repair:</B>
Damaged things can be moved from city to city by traders/merchants but are not sellable and are useless in their damaged state.  They must be repaired at a factory or trashed.  Repairing a damaged thing replaces it with a fully-functional and sellable thing.  Factories can repair one thing at a time and this requires both ore and labor.  Repairing can be done by a manufacturer at their own factory, or by any profession at a rented factory.</p>

<p><B>Renting:</B>
Any profession can rent a factory from a manufacturer in order to repair a damaged thing.  The renter will gain control of the factory in whichever city he rents it in and has until the contract is expired to repair as many things as he can with the ore and labor he is able to purchase and hire.  The renter must supply the ore and the workers.  If a repair is in progress when the rental expires, the ore and damaged thing will be refunded but the components applied are lost and worker contracts not affected.  </p>

<p><B>Canceling/Demolishing:</B>
Any factory task can be canceled before it has been completed.  This will result in the ore being refunded but the labor applied will be lost.  If a repair is canceled, the operator will receive the damaged thing back from the factory still fully damaged.  A built, idle factory can be demolished completely by its owner.  If demolished, the owner will receive a complete refund of the ore required to build the factory, but the components will be lost. </p>

